# nano syntax highlighting for lux.
#
# Unlike Vim or GtkSourceView, nano has no theme to inherit colours from — every
# colour is named here directly, so the same file has to look right on a black
# terminal and a white one, on whatever machine it lands on. Two rules follow
# from that:
#
#   1. Use nano's fixed mid-tone colour names (purple, teal, slate, ...), not the
#      basic eight (blue, brightblack, ...). The basic names are whatever each
#      terminal's theme decides they are — that is why "brightblack" comments can
#      vanish into a dark background. The named mid-tones map to fixed palette
#      slots and render the same everywhere.
#   2. Stay in the band that is legible on BOTH backgrounds — mid-luminance, no
#      colour so bright it washes out on white nor so dark it sinks into black.
#
# The palette is also colour-blind safe: no red, no green, distinguished by hue
# and lightness alone. Change any of it to taste; nano reloads on restart. (The
# named colours need a 256-colour terminal, which every graphical one is.)
#
# The mid-tone names (purple, teal, slate, ...) arrived in nano 5.0 (2020). An
# older nano rejects them with a "Mistakes in '.nanorc'" notice and shows no
# colour. Notably, macOS ships a pre-5.0 nano frozen at the last GPLv2 release —
# `brew install nano` gets a current one, and then this file just works.
#
# Install: copy to ~/.nano/lux.nanorc and add this line to ~/.nanorc:
#   include "~/.nano/lux.nanorc"
#
# Written by David M. Anderson with AI assistance. MIT, same as lux.

syntax lux "\.lux$"
comment "//"

# keywords and control flow
color purple "\<(let|var|if|else|while|for|in|func|return|struct|enum|match)\>"

# boolean values
color purple "\<(true|false)\>"

# built-in types
color teal "\<(int|float|bool|string|Option|Result|Output)\>"

# Option and Result values
color teal "\<(some|none|ok|err)\>"

# built-in functions
color rosy "\<(print|eprint|input|readLine|readFile|writeFile|args|run|length|parseInt|parseFloat)\>"

# numbers
color ocher "\<[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?\>"

# comments run // to end of line
color slate "//.*$"

# strings last, so a real string repaints over any keyword that appears inside it
color ocher ""([^"]|\\")*""

# the four escapes lux understands, highlighted within the string
color purple "\\[nt"\\]"
